<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Akathisia from aripiprazole (G25.71)</content>. Moderate akathisia after the dose increase: subjective restlessness with observed movement, without parkinsonism or dyskinesia. It is not a return of the psychosis. Akathisia is distressing and is linked with impulsive behavior and suicidal thoughts, so it is treated promptly. Asthma and a slow pulse, which would prevent propranolol, are absent. A benzodiazepine is avoided because of his alcohol use. [S]<list><item>Return aripiprazole to 10 mg daily from tomorrow.</item><item>Propranolol 10 mg twice daily for a few weeks.</item><item>Cut coffee to one cup in the morning.</item><item>Phone check from the care coordinator in three days.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">First episode of psychosis, provisional diagnosis (F29)</content>. No voices since the increase; the lower dose may let them return, so he and the team will watch for them. Safety reviewed: no thoughts of suicide or self-harm; the safety plan and the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline are in his phone, and he knows akathisia can make him feel desperate and to call if it does. [S]<list><item>If night-time voices return on 10 mg, the team will review the dose at the six-week visit rather than raise it now.</item><item>Six-week visit as planned.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Alcohol use disorder on naltrexone (F10.20)</content>. One or two drinks on three evenings this week; he has not used alcohol to calm the restlessness. [S]<list><item>Continue naltrexone and thiamine.</item><item>No alcohol to treat the restlessness or to get to sleep.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: The American Psychiatric Association Practice Guideline for the Treatment of Patients With Schizophrenia, Third Edition (2020); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Care coordinator phone check in three days. Six-week visit in two weeks, or sooner if the restlessness does not ease within a week or if voices or dark thoughts return. Next scheduled visit 2026-03-02: six-week review: symptoms, side effects, alcohol, sleep, and test results. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
